<Base title="Sources and methodology" description="How TheTravelFact chooses a station, a source, and a date, and what was left off.">
  <article class="shell page prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">Methodology</p>
    <h1>How a number gets on the page</h1>
    <p>The first twenty sheets were checked 14 November 2026. Milan through Japan, and then Tokyo through Croatia, were checked 15 November 2026. A fact is a sentence plus a URL plus a date. The date is the day we read the source, not the day the climate period ended.</p>
    <h2>URL scheme</h2>
    <p>One place, one URL. A country is <code>/mexico/</code>. A city is <code>/mexico/cancun/</code>. The same pattern is used for Canada, the United Kingdom, Italy, France, the Dominican Republic, Spain, Germany, Japan, Ireland, Portugal, the Netherlands, Greece, Iceland, Switzerland, Austria, Czechia, and Croatia. Months stay in the table. They do not get twelve extra URLs.</p>
    <h2>Climate</h2>
    <p>Mexico uses Servicio Meteorológico Nacional normals for 1991–2020, issued 6 November 2026, one station file per place. Canada uses Environment and Climate Change Canada's 1991–2020 composite normals. The United Kingdom uses Met Office 1991–2020 location averages, except that the UK sheet is the HadUK-Grid national block printed on that page, not Heathrow. Italy, France, Spain, Germany, the Dominican Republic, Japan, Ireland, the Netherlands, Iceland, Switzerland, Austria, Czechia, and Croatia use the WMO 1991–2020 normals distributed by NOAA NCEI, one named station per sheet. Kyoto is not in that Japanese submission, so its sheet uses the Japan Meteorological Agency's own 1991–2020 normal. Portugal is not in the NCEI collection. Its sheet uses IPMA's 1991–2020 fiche and says the fiche calls the series hybrid. Greece is not in the collection either. Greece and Athens use the Hellenic National Meteorological Service table for Elliniko and print the period on that page, 1955–2010. Santorini is not on the South Aegean list of that service, so its climate cells stay blank. Where a month is blank in a file, the cell is blank. Where a temperature row is tagged with another station's number, or matches another station exactly while the rest of the file does not, that row is left off.</p>
    <p>We do not name a best month. The page restates the highest high, the lowest low, and the heaviest and lightest water-column months, and leaves the choice to the reader.</p>
    <h2>The other facts</h2>
    <p>Plugs and voltage are the IEC world-plugs country lines. The 15 November lines for the new countries were read from the 16 August 2026 archive of that page, because a direct download did not return it. Driving is a statute or a government page, quoted in our own sentences. Greece uses Article 20 of Law 5209/2025. Iceland uses Article 18 of the 2019 traffic act. Tipping is a tourism office, and only when that page states a practice. Ireland's line is the Ireland.com taxi page. Holland.com, Visit Greece, Switzerland Tourism, VisitCzechia, and Croatia.hr state a practice and are quoted. Austria and Iceland still have no such page on this pass. Tap water is whatever CDC's traveler page actually says. Ireland's page is more specific than the others and is quoted. The rest of the new countries, like France, Spain, Germany, and Japan, do not get a yes or a no. Currency names and codes are ISO 4217, maintained by SIX. Time zone names are IANA's. Where zone1970.tab has folded a country's traditional name into another city's zone, the sheet prints the name in zone.tab and cites the link in the backward file. Entry requirements are a link, not a matrix.</p>
    <h2 id="gaps">Left off on purpose</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Toronto (City) rainfall is blank for April through October, so the sheet prints precipitation instead.</li>
      <li>Vancouver Harbour has no rainfall element. The sheet prints precipitation and does not borrow the airport.</li>
      <li>No tipping percentage for Toronto, Vancouver, Banff, or for Canada as a whole. Mexico's page says tipping is voluntary because that is what Profeco wrote, not a percent.</li>
      <li>Venice Tessera's March average high is blank in the WMO file.</li>
      <li>Roma Ciampino is missing summer precipitation, so Rome uses Fiumicino.</li>
      <li>Puerto Vallarta station 14116 has no 1991–2020 normal. The sheet uses El Cuale.</li>
      <li>Ringway, the old Manchester Airport station, closed in 2004. The sheet uses Woodford.</li>
      <li>Naples Capodichino leaves May and July temperatures, and July precipitation, blank.</li>
      <li>Punta Cana's maximum-temperature row is numbered as Santo Domingo, and its minimums match Santo Domingo's. Those temperatures are not printed. Precipitation is.</li>
      <li>Japan's IEC line lists both 50 hertz and 60 hertz and does not assign a city, so the sheet does not either.</li>
      <li>Kyoto is not in the NOAA Japan submission. Its numbers are the JMA normal, and the position is the OSCAR record.</li>
      <li>Portugal's IPMA fiche says the 1991–2020 series is hybrid, including WRF-modelled values. The numbers are printed with that caveat.</li>
      <li>Greece and Athens use Elliniko, 1955–2010, and say so. Santorini is not on the HNMS South Aegean list that was opened, so its climate cells stay blank.</li>
      <li>Zürich/Fluntern's file has no number-of-years row. The monthly values are still the ones in the file.</li>
      <li>No tourism-office tipping line was opened for Austria or Iceland.</li>
      <li>The full list, including a Tacubaya file that disagrees with an older WMO copy, is in the repository file GAPS.md.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>
</Base>
